p.src:
Normalization does not remove every redundancy. To see what remains, write a complex number in polar form, $z=x+iy=re^{i\chi}
  =r(\cos\chi+i\sin\chi).$ Multiplication multiplies moduli and adds angles: $r_1e^{i\chi_1}r_2e^{i\chi_2}
  =r_1r_2e^{i(\chi_1+\chi_2)}.$ Complex conjugation reflects the number across the real axis, $z^*=re^{-i\chi}.$ A complex number of unit modulus is a pure phase: $e^{i\chi},
  \qquad
  e^{i\chi}e^{-i\chi}=1.$ The word phase may refer either to the angle $\chi$ or to the unit complex number $e^{i\chi}$; the context tells us which is intended.

